# Administrative History: Gettysburg National Military Park and National Cemetery (NPS; Unrau, 1991 as listed on the npshistory.com index).
# Read: PDF pp. 6-8 (executive summary), 37, 60, 66, 70, 72, 103, 135 (dates of the first monuments, GBMA and park-commission works, 1925 description of walls).
# Everything here dates from 1864 onward: these are the dates before which a monument, avenue, tower or tablet must NOT be drawn.
@ NPS-ADHI
6 | later | Soldiers' National Monument dedicated July 1, 1869 | - | !1869 | stated | The Soldiers' National Monument in the cemetery was dedicated on July 1, 1869, with Maj. Gen. Meade as principal speaker | Soldiers' National Monument was dedicated on July 1,1869, with Major General George G. Meade as principal speaker.
7 | later | Warren survey of 1868-69 | R12.015 | ~1868-1869 survey (later than the battle) | stated | The battlefield was surveyed and mapped under Brevet Maj. Gen. G. K. Warren in 1868-69 for military instruction and for the Official Records atlas | The battlefield was surveyed and mapped under the direction of Brevet Major General G.K. Warren
7 | monuments | First monuments on the battlefield 1878-79 | - | !1878-1879 | stated | The first monuments on the battlefield were erected in 1878-79 | The first monuments on the Gettysburg battlefield were erected in 1878-79.
7 | monuments | GAR marble tablet for Strong Vincent on Little Round Top, 1878 | - | !1878 | stated | In summer 1878 the Pennsylvania GAR put a marble tablet on Little Round Top where Strong Vincent was mortally wounded, the first monument of any kind on the battlefield outside the cemetery | During a reunion encampment on East Cemetery Hill during the summer of 1878 >>> outside the cemetery
7 | monuments | 2nd Massachusetts monument near Spangler's Spring, 1879 | - | !1879 | stated | In summer 1879 the 2nd Massachusetts Infantry placed a bronze and granite monument near Spangler's Spring, the first regimental monument on the battlefield | The following summer the 2d Massachusetts Infantry placed a bronze and granite monument near Spangler's Spring
7 | later | GBMA purchase of Meade's headquarters house, 1887, and rules for monuments | bld-leister-house | !1887 | stated | In 1887 the GBMA bought the house Meade used as headquarters, decided that only bronze or granite monuments would be allowed, chose avenue names and ruled that monuments go where regiments stood in the line of battle | In 1887, for instance, the GBMA purchased the house used as headquarters by Meade >>> in the line of battle.
7 | monuments | 133 regimental monuments dedicated in 1888 | - | !1888 | stated | The 25th-anniversary ceremonies of July 3-4, 1888 included the dedication of 133 regimental monuments | The ceremonies featured a reenactment of Pickett's Charge and dedication of 133 regimental monuments.
8 | later | State of the park in 1895: 522 acres, 17 miles of avenues, 320 monuments | - | !1895 | stated | When the War Department took over in 1895 the battlefield included 522 acres, about 17 miles of avenues (many still dirt car paths) and 320 monuments | which then included 522 acres, some 17 miles of avenues, many of which were still dirt car paths, and 320 monuments.
8 | monuments | Iron markers for the US Regulars in the 1890s and Regular Army monuments of 1907-08 | - | !1890s-1909 | stated | Iron markers were set at the US Regulars' positions in the 1890s; Regular Army monuments and a large obelisk were erected in 1907-08 and dedicated May 31, 1909 | Iron markers had been erected at each of the positions of the U.S. Regulars >>> dedicated with ceremonies featuring President William H. Taft as speaker.
37 | monuments | First Minnesota Memorial of 1867 | - | !1867 | conflict | The report says the 1st Minnesota Memorial of 1867 was the first monument or memorial in both the national cemetery and the battlefield, two years before the Soldiers' National Monument, while elsewhere it calls the 1878 Vincent tablet the first monument outside the cemetery | In 1867 the first monument/memorial in both the national cemetery and battlefield was the 1st Minnesota Memorial. >>> two years.
66 | monuments | Massachusetts first state to appropriate money for battlefield monuments, 1883 | - | !1883 | stated | In 1883 Massachusetts gave the GBMA $5,000 and appropriated $500 for each of its monuments, the first state to fund monuments on the battlefield | During 1883 Massachusetts contributed a $5,000 appropriation to the GBMA. >>> upon the battlefield.
70 | monuments | Maryland Confederate monument, November 19, 1886 | - | !1886 | stated | The Maryland monument dedicated on the battlefield on November 19, 1886 was the first monument marking the position of a Confederate command | the first monument marking the position of a Confederate command at Gettysburg.
72 | woodlots | Ziegler's Grove denuded; GBMA decision to plant 125 trees | GZ-004 | 1880s (denuded after the battle) | stated | The GBMA decided to plant 125 trees on the denuded part of Ziegler's Grove to restore it as nearly as possible to its condition during the battle, but did not act; the War Department park commission later replanted denuded parts of the battlefield | It was decided to plant 125 trees on the denuded portion of Ziegler's Grove >>> replanted denuded portions of the battlefield.
103 | later | Park commission telfordized avenues, 1895-1905 | - | !1895-1905 | stated | In 1895-1905 the park commission turned the muddy GBMA cowpaths into over twenty miles of semipermanent telfordized avenues | Relying on the labor of Italian immigrants, local artisans, and contractors >>> telfordized" avenues
103 | later | Park commission towers, cannon and fencing, 1895-1905 | - | !1895-1905 | stated | In 1895-1905 more than 300 condemned cannon were mounted, five steel observation towers were built and more than 25 miles of boundary and battlefield fencing was constructed | More than 300 condemned cannon were mounted >>> as well as 13 miles of gutter paving.
103 | fences | Stone walls restored or rebuilt and trees planted, 1895-1905 | - | 1895-1905 restoration (rebuilt walls; later than 1863) | stated | In excess of five miles of stone walls were restored or rebuilt and nearly 17,000 trees were planted in denuded parts of the field, including Ziegler's Grove, Pitzer's Woods, Trostle Woods and Biesecker Woods | In excess of five miles of stone walls were restored or rebuilt >>> Trostle Woods, and Biesecker Woods.
103 | earthworks | Defense works resodded, relaid and rebuilt, 1895-1905 | - | 1895-1905 rebuilt works (later than 1863) | stated | The park commission resodded, relaid and rebuilt defense works where necessary, so surviving works are not unaltered 1863 profiles | Defense works were resodded, relaid, and rebuilt where necessary.
135 | later | 840 monuments on the battlefield in 1925 | - | !1925 | stated | The 1925 description by engineer Cope counted 840 monuments on the battlefield | The superintendent went on to note that there were 840 monuments on the battlefield.
135 | earthworks | Culp's Hill earthworks and East Cemetery Hill lunettes in 1925 | E-05,E-12 | 1925 statement (works rebuilt 1895-1905) | stated | In 1925 a line of earthworks thrown up by the Twelfth Corps survived on Culp's Hill and a number of lunettes at the Union battery positions on East Cemetery Hill | There is a line of earth-works on Culp's Hill >>> positions held by the Union batteries.
135 | fences | Hancock Avenue stone wall in 1925: 'practically the same as at the time of the battle' | P-01,FEN-cemetery-ridge-walls | 1925 statement (wall restored 1895-1905) | stated | The 1925 description says the stone wall on the west side of Hancock Avenue, from the Taneytown road to south of the Angle, is well preserved and practically the same as at the time of the battle | The stone wall along the west side of Hancock Avenue >>> practically the same as at the time of the battle.
135 | fences | Little Round Top, Big Round Top and Seminary Ridge walls in 1925 | P-14 | 1925 statement (walls restored 1895-1905) | stated | In 1925 stone walls on the south side of Little Round Top were described as erected and used by Union forces, long lines at the base of Big Round Top as erected and used by Confederates, and a long line along Seminary Ridge as used by Confederates | There are some stone walls on the south side of Little Round Top >>> used by the Confederates and is well preserved.
